# Working notes: plans, critiques, reviews, orchestration prompts.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A SAFETY PATTERN, NOT TIDINESS. These 59 root-level files are
# untracked but not ignored, so `git add -A` sweeps every one of them into a
# commit on a PUBLIC repo. That has now happened twice, caught both times only
# because someone read the file list before pushing. A near-miss caught twice by
# attention is a hazard, not a track record.
#
# Scoped to the root (`/`) and to the naming vocabulary these documents actually
# use — deliberately NOT `/*.md` with an allowlist. A blanket ignore would
# silently swallow a future CONTRIBUTING.md or SECURITY.md that someone meant to
# commit, and a file missing from a commit is harder to notice than a file
# wrongly present in one. Tracked root docs (README, ARCHITECTURE, CHANGELOG)
# match none of these patterns.
#
# ⚠️ The pattern reduces the blast radius; it does not remove it. A planning doc
# with novel naming is still untracked-and-unignored. The actual fix is to STAGE
# EXPLICIT PATHS — never `git add -A` or `git add .` in a repo that carries
# untracked working notes.
/FORECASTER-*.md
/*-CRITIQUE-*.md
/*-critique-*.md
/*-PLAN-V*.md
/*-REVIEW-*.md
/*-BRIEFING.md
/*-HANDOFF.md

# mutation testing (Stryker) — reports are measurements, not artifacts.
# A crashed run also leaves full copies of mutated source in .stryker-tmp,
# which ESLint would otherwise lint.
reports/mutation/
.stryker-tmp/
